WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.402
SNC-Lavalin: the scandal that exposed political interference in criminal prosecution.

00:00:05.352 --> 00:00:16.772
In 2019, the Prime Minister's Office pressured Attorney General Jody Wilson-Raybould to offer SNC-Lavalin a Deferred Prosecution Agreement instead of criminal charges for bribery and fraud in Libya.

00:00:16.772 --> 00:00:19.170
She refused and was demoted.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:23.670
The Ethics Commissioner found the PM violated the Conflict of Interest Act.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:27.409
No criminal charges were laid against any political actor.

00:00:27.409 --> 00:00:30.227
Timeline of the SNC-Lavalin affair.

00:00:30.227 --> 00:00:41.829
From the original RCMP investigation in 2015 through the political pressure campaign, the AG's resignation, the Ethics Commissioner ruling, and the 2019 election consequences.

00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:47.420
The Ethics Commissioner found the Prime Minister contravened Section 9 of the Conflict of Interest Act.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:51.386
This was the first finding of a sitting PM violating the Act.

00:00:51.386 --> 00:00:54.852
Despite the finding, no criminal charges were laid.

00:00:54.852 --> 00:00:58.784
The PM was re-elected in October 2019.

00:00:58.784 --> 00:01:12.079
The SNC-Lavalin affair establishes a pattern of political interference in prosecution that repeats across TENET5 investigations: the PMO pressures independent officers, the ethics system documents the violation, but no criminal consequences follow.

00:01:12.079 --> 00:01:18.852
The same pattern appears in the RCMP Commissioner Lucki interference with the Nova Scotia mass casualty investigation.
